From c34559574b255f1e6f5f23726485840700ef7be4 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Thu, 13 Aug 2026 21:37:41 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 09107/11362] Give ARC a number that can move, and let measured
 wins turn themselves on

REQ-ARC-BENCH-6267 and REQ-ARC-FLAG-LEDGER-6268. Operator asked how to make the
ARC effort self-improving unattended. Measuring the loop first found it had
generation and no selection.

WHAT THE MEASUREMENT SHOWED. Over the last 10 milestones the loop ran 16 ARC
tasks and 13 ended `ready_no_solve_claim` or `default_off` -- every one shipped a
capability behind a flag and turned nothing on. There are 101 distinct
`CARNOT_ARC_*` flags and no record anywhere of which are on, why, or on what
evidence. Three tasks in that window are named "holdout" -- exp6295, exp6308,
exp6401 -- and their metric sets share ZERO keys. Milestone .542 and .550 are not
comparable, so nothing could tell whether the agent got better.

The cause is structural. `reproducible_total_levels` hit 183 of 183 on
2026-07-17. Every public game is cleared and adaptered, so the metric that
steered this work for months is pinned forever. Nothing replaced it, and
readiness scaffolding is what remains measurable when the real metric cannot
move.

1 -- THE BENCHMARK, defined as the scored metric rather than a proxy.
`scripts/arc_bench.py` runs the adapter-free path (the same first-contact
mechanism the live agent uses on an unseen game) and reports levels cleared
against actions spent. Making the benchmark BE the metric removes the
calibration problem: there is roughly one scored submission on record, so no
proxy could have been validated against it anyway.

Verified to discriminate before the file was written. At 3000 expansions: ls20
level 1 in 17,197 actions, ft09 level 1 in 8,768, vc33 level 1 in 232, ka59 and
dc22 level 0. Three of five clear, and spend ranges by two orders of magnitude.
A constant would have been useless.

Actions are counted by wrapping `env.step`, not read from the returned solution
length. On ls20 those are 17,197 and 13. The competition scores what was spent.

2 -- THE LEDGER. `scripts/arc_flag_ledger.py` plus `ops/arc_flag_ledger.yaml`:
flag, state, the evidence that justifies it, the promotion date. `--discover`
reads flag names from agent source rather than a maintained list, for the reason
this project keeps relearning -- a hand-written watch-list drifts narrower than
what it watches. It found 95 and every one is `unevaluated`, which is the
condition being ended.

Independence comes from GAMES, not repeats. `graph_explore_solve_v2` is
deterministic: dc22 spent exactly 11,737 actions on two separate runs. Averaging
repeats would manufacture confidence from a sample of one. Anyone adding
stochasticity to the search has to revisit that rule.

3 -- THE REGRESSION GATE, and it is not optional. Any single game losing a level
REFUSES the promotion and names the game, even when the aggregate improves. The
precedent is on the record: the ARC engine store overwrote unconditionally and
destroyed ka59 from 1.0 to 0.0, retention worth p=4.9e-5. An average is exactly
the statistic that hides that. The test for this constructs an arm that clears
three new games and loses one, asserts the aggregate really is better, and
asserts it is refused anyway. Proven to bite by mutation.

4 -- WIRED into milestone close beside the five existing audits, with
`--discover` alongside it so a flag shipped this milestone is tracked from the
milestone it lands in. Non-fatal, never blocks.

19 tests. The named limitation, printed in every report rather than buried:
these are the 25 PUBLIC games with adapters bypassed. Disabling the adapter
removes the hand-written route, not the knowledge that produced it. It is the
best held-out surface that exists here and it is still a proxy for a hidden
game. No number from this file may be quoted as a hidden-game result.

